  "id": "Compound::DB06772 - Disease::DOID:10283",
  "verbalization": "Cabazitaxel is implicated in the pathogenesis of prostate cancer through multiple interconnected mechanisms. The evidence suggests that Cabazitaxel, through its inclusion in the class of Microtubule Inhibitors, directly contributes to the treatment of prostate cancer, as Docetaxel, a closely related compound, is known to treat prostate cancer. This relationship is further supported by the fact that Cabazitaxel causes Hydronephrosis and Neutropenic Enterocolitis, both of which are conditions that can be associated with prostate cancer, though the direct causal link is not explicitly stated. The shared lowest common ancestor of Cabazitaxel and Docetaxel, which is the Tetracyclic Diterpenoid, suggests a common pharmacological pathway. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Cabazitaxel to influence prostate cancer through its role in microtubule inhibition, which is a well-established mechanism in cancer treatment. The evidence, while not definitively establishing a direct causal link, provides a coherent framework that connects Cabazitaxel to prostate cancer through its pharmacological properties and associated conditions.",
